__d(function(a,e,t,i,n,s,h){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2013 History",textbook:"Our Pasts \u2013 I",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"New Questions and Ideas",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand why new religious and philosophical ideas emerged in ancient India around 600 BCE","Identify the key teachings of the Buddha \u2014 the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path","Describe the life story of Siddhartha Gautama and how he became the Buddha","Explain the teachings of Mahavira and the core principles of Jainism","Distinguish between the teachings of Buddhism and Jainism and identify common themes","Understand the concept of the Upanishads and how they challenged older Vedic rituals","Appreciate why both teachers emphasised non-violence (ahimsa) and truth (satya)","Connect the social and political context of the Mahajanapada era to the rise of new ideas"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Understanding of the Vedic varna system and Brahmin ritual dominance from Chapter 5","Knowledge of the Mahajanapadas and the gana/sangha republics from Chapter 6","Awareness that both the Buddha and Mahavira came from gana/sangha territories"],introduction:{why_important:"Around 2,500 years ago, India underwent a revolution of ideas. People began questioning age-old rituals, the authority of priests, and the nature of suffering and liberation. The teachings that emerged \u2014 Buddhism and Jainism \u2014 spread across Asia and still guide the lives of hundreds of millions of people today. Understanding these ideas helps you understand not just ancient history, but living world religions.",real_life_relevance:"When you practise kindness, avoid harming others, and try to control your desires \u2014 you are acting on ideas first articulated by the Buddha and Mahavira 2,500 years ago. These ancient Indian thinkers shaped the ethics of an entire civilisation. India's national emblem (the Ashoka Chakra) and the national motto 'Satyameva Jayate' (Truth Alone Triumphs) are both rooted in the philosophical revolution this chapter describes.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka has a deep connection to both Buddhism and Jainism. The town of Shravanabelagola in Hassan district is one of the holiest Jain pilgrimage sites in the world \u2014 home to the 57-foot monolithic statue of Gommateshwara (Bahubali), erected in 981 CE. Jainism flourished in Karnataka under the Ganga dynasty. Buddhism also had a presence \u2014 the Buddhist site at Brahmagiri and Sannati (Gulbarga district) where a large stupa and Ashokan inscriptions were discovered. Karnataka's intellectual heritage is deeply shaped by these ancient questioning traditions.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 6 showed that both the Buddha and Mahavira came from gana/sangha republics. This chapter explains WHAT they taught and WHY their ideas were revolutionary. The Mahajanapada era's growing cities, trade, and social complexity created the conditions in which new questions about life, suffering, and justice could flourish."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Siddhartha Gautama (the Buddha)",role:"Prince of the Shakya clan who renounced his royal life, sought enlightenment, and became the Buddha ('the awakened one'). Founded Buddhism."},{name:"Mahavira (Vardhamana)",role:"Prince of the Vajji confederacy who renounced royal life, practised extreme asceticism, and attained enlightenment. Became the 24th Tirthankara of Jainism."},{name:"Upanishadic thinkers",role:"Ancient Indian philosophers (including women like Gargi) who questioned the purpose of rituals and explored the nature of the self (atman) and ultimate reality (Brahman)."},{name:"Gargi",role:"A female philosopher mentioned in the Upanishads who engaged in philosophical debates with great scholars \u2014 showing women's intellectual participation in ancient India."}],plot:["By around 600 BCE, growing cities, trade, and social complexity created new questions: Why is there suffering? Do expensive rituals really help? Is birth-based social hierarchy fair?","The Upanishads were philosophical texts (composed ~800\u2013200 BCE) that questioned the purpose of Vedic rituals and explored deeper truths about the self (atman) and ultimate reality (Brahman)","Thinkers in the Upanishads argued that inner knowledge, not ritual, leads to liberation \u2014 a significant challenge to Brahmin authority","Siddhartha Gautama was born a prince in the Shakya gana (Lumbini, Nepal) around 563 BCE","Sheltered from suffering his whole life, he was shocked when he first saw old age, sickness, and death \u2014 called the 'Three Sights'","He renounced his royal life, wife, and son to seek answers about suffering \u2014 became a wandering ascetic","After years of practice, he attained enlightenment (bodhi) under a peepal tree at Bodhgaya \u2014 becoming the 'Buddha' (awakened one)","He delivered his first sermon at Sarnath (Deer Park) \u2014 explaining the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path","He taught in Prakrit (the common people's language), welcomed people of all varnas into his sangha (community), and emphasised ahimsa (non-violence)","Vardhamana Mahavira was born into the Licchavi clan of the Vajji confederacy around 540 BCE","He renounced royal life at 30, practised extreme asceticism (fasting, exposure to elements), and attained kaivalya (perfect knowledge) after 12 years","Mahavira became the 24th Tirthankara \u2014 a liberated teacher who shows others the path to liberation","Key Jain teachings: ahimsa (non-violence to ALL living beings), truth, non-stealing, non-possession, and celibacy","Both the Buddha and Mahavira challenged the caste system, the authority of Brahmin rituals, and the use of expensive sacrifices"],moral:"Great questions about suffering, equality, and truth \u2014 asked by ordinary people and great thinkers alike \u2014 can transform societies. The courage to question accepted traditions and seek inner truth is at the heart of human progress."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"The Buddha's Teachings \u2014 Four Noble Truths and Eightfold Path",simple_explanation:"The Buddha taught that suffering exists in life, it has a cause (desire), it can be ended, and there is a path to end it. That path is the Eightfold Path \u2014 eight right ways of thinking, speaking, and living.",detailed_explanation:"After attaining enlightenment at Bodhgaya, the Buddha gave his first sermon at the Deer Park in Sarnath. He taught the Four Noble Truths (Arya Satyas): (1) Dukkha \u2014 suffering exists in life; (2) Samudaya \u2014 suffering arises from desire (tanha/craving); (3) Nirodha \u2014 suffering can end if desire is overcome; (4) Magga \u2014 there is a path (the Eightfold Path) to overcome desire and end suffering. The Eightfold Path (Arya Ashtangika Marga) consists of: Right Understanding, Right Intention, Right Speech, Right Action, Right Livelihood, Right Effort, Right Mindfulness, and Right Concentration. The Buddha described this as the Middle Way (Madhyama Pratipad) \u2014 avoiding both extreme luxury and extreme self-denial. He emphasised: ahimsa (non-violence), karuna (compassion), and pragya (wisdom). He taught in Prakrit (not Sanskrit) so common people could understand. He accepted monks and nuns from all varnas into his sangha \u2014 directly challenging caste discrimination.",real_life_example:"Think about a time when you really wanted something \u2014 a new phone, first rank, or a friend's approval \u2014 and felt miserable when you didn't get it. The Buddha's teaching says: the misery came from the craving, not from not getting the thing. If you can reduce the craving itself, the suffering also reduces. This is why practising contentment and mindfulness \u2014 ideas from Buddhist teaching \u2014 are recommended by modern psychologists too.",karnataka_example:"The Dharmasthala temple in Dakshina Kannada district is managed by the Heggade family and famously welcomes people of all religions and castes. This inclusive spirit directly echoes the Buddha's teaching that the sangha is open to all regardless of birth. Karnataka's Mahamastakabhisheka festival at Shravanabelagola \u2014 when thousands bathe the Gomateshwara statue \u2014 is one of the largest religious gatherings in India, rooted in Jain/Buddhist traditions of non-violence and renunciation.",common_mistakes:"Students often list the Four Noble Truths as just 'suffering, desire, end of suffering, path' \u2014 but they must understand what each means. Also, many students think the Buddha said 'life is only suffering' \u2014 this is wrong. He said suffering EXISTS in life, but it CAN BE ended. He was ultimately optimistic \u2014 there IS a way out.",memory_tip:"4 Noble Truths = DSEN: Dukkha (suffering exists), Samudaya (source is desire), End is possible (Nirodha), Noble 8-fold path (Magga). Remember: 'Dukh Se Nikal' (Get out of suffering) \u2014 DSEN!"},{concept_name:"Mahavira and Jain Teachings",simple_explanation:"Mahavira taught that the only way to be free from the cycle of birth and death is to live a life of complete non-violence, truth, and giving up all attachments. Every living being \u2014 even the tiniest insect \u2014 has a soul and deserves not to be harmed.",detailed_explanation:"Vardhamana Mahavira (540\u2013468 BCE) was born into the Licchavi clan of the Vajji confederacy. He renounced royal life at age 30 and practised extreme asceticism for 12 years \u2014 fasting severely, meditating in difficult postures, and enduring heat and cold without complaint. He attained kaivalya (perfect knowledge/enlightenment) and became the 24th Tirthankara \u2014 one who has 'crossed over' the ocean of rebirth and shows others how to cross too. The five main vows of Jainism are: ahimsa (non-violence to ALL living beings), satya (truth), asteya (non-stealing), brahmacharya (celibacy), and aparigraha (non-possession). Jain philosophy holds that every living being \u2014 including plants, insects, and microorganisms \u2014 has a soul (jiva) and therefore must not be harmed. The universe is eternal and not created by a god. Karma (actions) bind the soul to the cycle of rebirth; only complete renunciation and non-violence can liberate the soul (moksha). Jain monks follow extremely strict rules \u2014 some even sweep the ground before walking to avoid crushing insects.",real_life_example:"Think of Mahatma Gandhi's philosophy of non-violence (ahimsa) in the Indian freedom struggle. Gandhi was deeply inspired by Jain teachings \u2014 he grew up in Gujarat surrounded by Jain merchants and thinkers. When he said 'an eye for an eye makes the whole world blind', he was echoing Mahavira's teaching that responding to violence with more violence only creates more suffering and karma.",karnataka_example:"Shravanabelagola in Hassan district, Karnataka, is the most important Jain pilgrimage site in South India. The 57-foot monolithic statue of Gommateshwara (Bahubali) \u2014 erected in 981 CE during the reign of the Western Ganga dynasty \u2014 stands as one of the world's tallest free-standing statues. The Mahamastakabhisheka ceremony (head-anointing), held every 12 years, attracts millions. Karnataka's Ganga dynasty kings were devout Jains who patronised Jain art, literature, and learning \u2014 making Karnataka one of Jainism's most important historical centres.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse Buddhism and Jainism. Key difference: Jainism is MORE extreme in ahimsa \u2014 Jain monks/nuns filter drinking water, wear masks to avoid inhaling insects, and sweep paths. Buddhism's Middle Way avoids such extremes. Also, Jainism does NOT believe in a creator god; Buddhism also does not focus on god, but emphasises the Buddha's path. Neither is theistic in the way Hinduism or Christianity is.",memory_tip:"Mahavira's 5 Vows = AS-AB-A: Ahimsa (non-violence), Satya (truth), Asteya (non-stealing), Brahmacharya (celibacy), Aparigraha (non-possession). Remember 'AS-AB-A' \u2014 Mahavira's 5-point checklist for liberation!"},{concept_name:"The Upanishads and Questioning Vedic Rituals",simple_explanation:"While some people were composing more rituals, others were asking deeper questions: What is the self? What is the universe made of? Is ritual the only path to truth? The Upanishads are texts that record these daring philosophical questions and their answers.",detailed_explanation:"The Upanishads (literally 'sitting near a teacher') are philosophical texts composed between approximately 800\u2013200 BCE. They represent a major shift in Indian thought \u2014 away from external ritual and toward internal knowledge. While the earlier Vedic tradition emphasised correct performance of sacrifices (yajna) as the path to divine favour, Upanishadic thinkers asked: What is the ultimate reality? What is the self (atman)? What is the relationship between the individual self and the universal reality (Brahman)? The key teaching of many Upanishads is that atman (the individual soul) and Brahman (the universal/cosmic reality) are ultimately ONE \u2014 a teaching called Advaita (non-duality). This meant: liberation does not come from ritual but from inner knowledge (jnana). Remarkably, the Upanishads include female thinkers: Gargi Vachaknavi was a philosopher who debated with the great sage Yajnavalkya in the royal court. The Upanishads deeply influenced later Hindu philosophy, Buddhism, and Jainism \u2014 all of which questioned ritual-only approaches to liberation.",real_life_example:"Think of a student who scores 100% on every exam by only memorising answers without understanding them. They follow all the 'rituals' of studying but have no real knowledge. Now think of a student who asks 'why?' about everything, understands deeply, and develops wisdom that lasts. The Upanishadic shift was exactly this \u2014 from ritual performance (like memorising) to genuine understanding (like real wisdom).",karnataka_example:"Adi Shankaracharya (788\u2013820 CE), born in Kerala but who established four mathas (monasteries) across India including Sringeri in Karnataka, was the greatest interpreter of the Upanishads. His philosophy of Advaita Vedanta ('non-dual reality') \u2014 that the individual self (atman) and the universal reality (Brahman) are one \u2014 is directly based on Upanishadic thought. The Sringeri Sharada Peetha in Chikkamagaluru district, Karnataka, established by Shankaracharya, remains India's most important centre of Advaita Vedanta learning \u2014 making Karnataka literally the home of the Upanishadic philosophical tradition.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse the Upanishads with the Vedas. The Upanishads are part of the broader Vedic literature (they are sometimes called the 'end of the Vedas' \u2014 Vedanta) but they represent a DEPARTURE from Vedic ritualism. Also, the Upanishads are philosophical texts, not hymns \u2014 they contain dialogues, debates, and arguments, not just prayers.",memory_tip:"Upanishad = 'Sitting NEAR a teacher' (upa = near, nishad = sitting). They are philosophical CONVERSATIONS \u2014 student sits near teacher and asks deep questions. From RITUAL \u2192 to REASON. That is the Upanishadic revolution!"}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Buddha",meaning:"'The awakened one' in Sanskrit/Pali. Title given to Siddhartha Gautama after he attained enlightenment under the peepal tree at Bodhgaya around 528 BCE."},{term:"Dukkha",meaning:"The Pali/Sanskrit word for suffering or dissatisfaction \u2014 the first of the Buddha's Four Noble Truths. The Buddha taught that suffering is an unavoidable part of existence."},{term:"Nirvana",meaning:"The state of liberation in Buddhism \u2014 the extinguishing of desire, hatred, and delusion, leading to freedom from the cycle of birth and death (samsara)."},{term:"Ahimsa",meaning:"Non-violence \u2014 the principle of not harming any living being through action, speech, or thought. Central to both Buddhism and Jainism; later adopted by Mahatma Gandhi."},{term:"Tirthankara",meaning:"Literally 'ford-maker' \u2014 one who has crossed the ocean of rebirth and shows others the path. Mahavira was the 24th and last Tirthankara in the Jain tradition."},{term:"Upanishads",meaning:"Philosophical texts composed around 800\u2013200 BCE that questioned the purpose of Vedic rituals and explored the nature of the self (atman) and ultimate reality (Brahman)."},{term:"Atman",meaning:"The individual soul or self in Indian philosophy. The Upanishads teach that atman is identical to Brahman (the universal reality) \u2014 a key insight leading to liberation."},{term:"Sangha",meaning:"Community or assembly. In Buddhism, the sangha is the community of monks, nuns, and lay followers \u2014 one of the 'Three Jewels' of Buddhism (Buddha, Dhamma, Sangha)."},{term:"Jina",meaning:"'Conqueror' in Sanskrit \u2014 one who has conquered desire and attachment. Mahavira was called Jina; his followers are called Jainas (Jains)."},{term:"Bodhgaya",meaning:"The place in Bihar where Siddhartha Gautama attained enlightenment under a peepal (Bodhi) tree, becoming the Buddha. It is the most sacred site in Buddhism today."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"Explain the Four Noble Truths of the Buddha in your own words.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: First Noble Truth \u2014 Dukkha: Life contains suffering. Old age, sickness, death, not getting what we want \u2014 these are all forms of dukkha (suffering/dissatisfaction).","Step 2: Second Noble Truth \u2014 Samudaya: Suffering has a cause. The cause is tanha \u2014 craving or desire. We suffer because we want things, cling to things, and fear losing things.","Step 3: Third Noble Truth \u2014 Nirodha: Suffering can end. If we can overcome craving, we can overcome suffering. Liberation (nirvana) is possible \u2014 this is the optimistic core of Buddhism.","Step 4: Fourth Noble Truth \u2014 Magga: There is a path to end suffering. The Eightfold Path (right understanding, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness, concentration) is that path.","Conclusion: The Four Noble Truths are a diagnosis-and-cure framework: identify the problem (suffering), find the cause (desire), know that a cure exists (nirvana), and follow the prescription (Eightfold Path)."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Use the word 'ahimsa' in a sentence connecting both Buddhist and Jain teachings.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall meaning \u2014 ahimsa means non-violence; not harming any living being through action, speech, or thought.","Step 2: Connect to both traditions \u2014 both the Buddha and Mahavira placed ahimsa at the centre of their teachings, though Jainism extended it to even the smallest organisms.","Step 3: Write sentence: 'Ahimsa \u2014 the principle of non-violence \u2014 was the shared moral foundation of both Buddhism and Jainism: while the Buddha advocated ahimsa as part of his Eightfold Path and refused to sanction animal sacrifice, Mahavira extended ahimsa to every living being, including plants and insects, making it the absolute cornerstone of Jain ethics.'"]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"How can the Buddha's concept of the Middle Way apply to a student's life today?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Explain the Middle Way \u2014 the Buddha taught that extreme luxury (overindulgence) and extreme self-denial (starvation/harsh asceticism) are both wrong. The Middle Way is a balanced, moderate path.","Step 2: Apply to student life \u2014 extreme studying with no rest leads to burnout; extreme leisure with no study leads to failure. The Middle Way for a student is a balanced schedule: regular study, adequate sleep, healthy food, some recreation.","Step 3: Extend the application \u2014 in relationships, extreme attachment to friendships leads to jealousy and heartbreak; extreme isolation leads to loneliness. A middle way means healthy relationships without unhealthy dependency.","Step 4: Conclusion \u2014 the Middle Way is one of the most practical philosophical concepts for any age. It teaches moderation, balance, and the wisdom to avoid extremes \u2014 a lesson that 2,500-year-old Buddhist teaching offers to every modern student."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"The Buddha's Teachings \u2014 Four Noble Truths and Eightfold Path",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Where did the Buddha attain enlightenment?",a:"The Buddha attained enlightenment under a peepal tree at Bodhgaya in Bihar."},{type:"Recall",q:"Where did the Buddha deliver his first sermon?",a:"The Buddha delivered his first sermon at the Deer Park in Sarnath (near Varanasi)."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why did the Buddha teach in Prakrit rather than Sanskrit?",a:"The Buddha taught in Prakrit \u2014 the language of ordinary people \u2014 so his teachings could be understood by everyone, regardless of their level of Vedic education. This was a deliberate choice to make his message accessible to all people, not just Brahmin scholars."},{type:"Understanding",q:"What is the 'Middle Way' in Buddhism?",a:"The Middle Way is the Buddha's teaching that one should avoid both extreme self-indulgence (luxury and pleasure-seeking) and extreme self-denial (harsh asceticism). A moderate, balanced life of discipline without suffering is the correct path."},{type:"Application",q:"How did both the Buddha and Mahavira challenge the varna/caste system?",a:"Both accepted followers from all varnas into their communities (sangha/order) without discrimination based on birth. Both criticised expensive Brahmin rituals and animal sacrifice. Both taught that liberation came from one's own actions and wisdom, not from the varna one was born into."}]}],key_takeaways:["Around 600 BCE, growing cities, trade, and social complexity created conditions for new religious and philosophical questioning.","The Upanishads (800\u2013200 BCE) questioned Vedic rituals and explored the self (atman) and ultimate reality (Brahman) \u2014 Gargi was a female philosopher in this tradition.","Siddhartha Gautama became the Buddha at Bodhgaya; gave his first sermon at Sarnath's Deer Park.","The Four Noble Truths: Dukkha (suffering), Samudaya (desire causes it), Nirodha (it can end), Magga (Eightfold Path is the way).","The Buddha taught the Middle Way \u2014 avoiding extremes of luxury and harsh asceticism.","The Buddha taught in Prakrit, welcomed all varnas into the sangha, and emphasised ahimsa.","Mahavira (24th Tirthankara) attained kaivalya after 12 years of asceticism; his 5 vows: ahimsa, satya, asteya, brahmacharya, aparigraha.","Jainism extends ahimsa to ALL living beings \u2014 even the smallest organisms have souls (jiva).","Both Buddhism and Jainism challenged Brahmin authority, expensive rituals, and birth-based discrimination.","Karnataka connection: Shravanabelagola (Jain pilgrimage site \u2014 Gomateshwara statue), Sringeri (Upanishadic Advaita centre)."],mind_map:{root:"New Questions and Ideas",branches:[{branch:"Upanishads",sub:["Questioning rituals","Atman = Brahman","Female thinker Gargi","Knowledge over ritual"]},{branch:"Buddhism",sub:["Siddhartha Gautama","Bodhgaya \u2013 enlightenment","4 Noble Truths (DSEN)","Eightfold Path","Middle Way","Sangha open to all"]},{branch:"Jainism",sub:["Mahavira \u2013 24th Tirthankara","Kaivalya after 12 years","5 Vows (AS-AB-A)","Ahimsa to ALL living beings","Shravanabelagola \u2013 Karnataka"]},{branch:"Common Themes",sub:["Ahimsa (non-violence)","Challenge to Brahmin rituals","Open to all varnas","Inner knowledge over ritual"]},{branch:"Karnataka Links",sub:["Shravanabelagola \u2013 Jain site","Sringeri \u2013 Upanishad centre","Sannati \u2013 Buddhist stupa","Ganga dynasty patronised Jainism"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 7 covers the rise of new philosophical and religious ideas in ancient India around 600\u2013400 BCE. Three major developments: (1) Upanishads \u2014 philosophical texts questioning Vedic rituals; argued that atman (self) = Brahman (universal reality); Gargi was a female Upanishadic philosopher. (2) Buddhism \u2014 Siddhartha Gautama (born Shakya gana, Lumbini) attained enlightenment at Bodhgaya under a peepal tree; gave first sermon at Sarnath. Four Noble Truths: Dukkha (suffering), Samudaya (desire is the cause), Nirodha (suffering can end), Magga (Eightfold Path is the way). Taught the Middle Way \u2014 avoid extremes. Taught in Prakrit; welcomed all varnas into the sangha. (3) Jainism \u2014 Mahavira (born Licchavi clan, Vajji confederacy); attained kaivalya after 12 years of asceticism; 24th Tirthankara. Five vows: ahimsa, satya, asteya, brahmacharya, aparigraha. Extended ahimsa to ALL living beings including plants. Both Buddhism and Jainism challenged the varna system, Brahmin ritual authority, and animal sacrifice. Karnataka connections: Shravanabelagola (Gomateshwara statue \u2014 Jain pilgrimage); Sringeri (Advaita Vedanta \u2014 Upanishadic philosophy centre).",must_remember_facts:["Upanishads: philosophical texts questioning rituals; atman = Brahman; Gargi was a female philosopher.","Buddha = Siddhartha Gautama; born Shakya gana (Lumbini, Nepal); clan: Kshatriya.","Enlightenment: Bodhgaya (Bihar) under peepal/Bodhi tree.","First sermon: Deer Park, Sarnath (near Varanasi).","Four Noble Truths: Dukkha, Samudaya, Nirodha, Magga (DSEN).","Middle Way: avoid both extreme luxury and extreme asceticism.","Buddha taught in Prakrit; accepted all varnas into sangha.","Mahavira: born Licchavi clan, Vajji confederacy; 24th Tirthankara.","Mahavira's 5 vows: ahimsa, satya, asteya, brahmacharya, aparigraha.","Karnataka: Shravanabelagola (Gomateshwara/Bahubali statue) \u2014 most important Jain site in South India."]}},2547,[]);